arguments.content:
{
  "behavior_in_task_description": {
    "outcome": "pass",
    "explanation": "instruction.md is deliberately written as a riddle-style bug report, but every f2p behavior maps to a sentence in it: three nested colors ('opened a colour, opened a second inside it, a third inside that... every letter comes out the same'), the hex-color equivalent ('Same however I write the colours out'), the by-name vs bare-closer equivalence ('same whether I close a tag by name or with the plain closer'), the crossed-order-closing rule ('Closing two of them in the opposite order... gives the wrong colour, where I'd expect the one closed first to be the one showing'), and the empty-span rule for tags with nothing under them, whether auto-closed or left open ('a tag that ends up with nothing under it, closed straight away or left hanging open at the end, still turns up as an entry in the list'). The screenshot supplies the concrete reproduction (exact markup string and the buggy Spans panel) that the prose intentionally omits, which is consistent with task.toml's difficulty_explanation stating the repro exists only in the screenshot. Nothing tested is left wholly unstated."
  },
  "behavior_in_tests": {
    "outcome": "pass",
    "explanation": "Every behavior narrated in instruction.md is exercised by tests/test_markup_nesting.py: nested colors (test_nested_colors_paint_each_character), hex-color nesting (test_nested_hex_colors_paint_each_character), name-vs-bare-closer equivalence and re-opening after a close (test_sibling_tag_inside_nesting_paints_each_character), innermost/crossed-order semantics (test_innermost_tag_wins_for_properly_nested_pair, test_first_closed_tag_wins_for_crossed_pair), and zero-width tags producing no spans whether auto-closed, name-closed, or left open (test_tags_that_wrap_nothing_produce_no_spans). p2p tests additionally pin down pre-existing behavior that must not regress. No narrated behavior is left unchecked."
  },
  "informative_test_structure": {
    "outcome": "pass",
    "explanation": "tests/test_markup_nesting.py is organized under clear section banners ('Nesting: the tag closest to a character is the one that shows.' and 'Regression guards: behaviour that already worked and must keep working.'), and every test function carries a docstring stating exactly what property it checks. test.sh and verifier.py are both heavily commented, explaining each stage (restore tests/, apply test.patch, install pytest offline, run suite, judge, compute reward) and the reasoning behind each anti-cheat measure."
  },
  "anti_cheating_measures": {
    "outcome": "pass",
    "explanation": "The Dockerfile strips .git after cloning and re-inits a single base commit (so the agent cannot inspect the fix PR/future history), and explicitly does not COPY tests/ or solution/ into the image. test.sh restores the entire tests/ tree from the immutable harbor_base tag and cleans untracked files before applying test.patch, preventing a planted conftest or stub from shortcutting the suite, and runs pytest with --noconftest and -o addopts= to close the same hole for a conftest planted elsewhere. It also clears any pre-existing reward/verdict files before scoring so a planted reward cannot survive. The rubric judge (judge.py) deliberately withholds fix.patch, test.patch, and the screenshots from both grading backends to avoid turning the judge into a similarity checker. Verified against upstream GitHub history that fix.patch is the genuine upstream fix (composed of PR #5829's follow-on 'edge cases' commits), not a fabricated shortcut."
  },
  "structured_data_schema": {
    "outcome": "not_applicable",
    "explanation": "This is a Python library bug-fix task (fixing src/textual/markup.py); the agent is not expected to produce structured output such as JSON/CSV/API responses. The JSON files present (rubrics.json, config.json, evidence_bundle.json) are grading infrastructure, not agent-facing output schemas."
  },
  "pinned_dependencies": {
    "outcome": "pass",
    "explanation": "The Dockerfile pins all installed Python packages to exact versions (markdown-it-py[plugins,linkify]==3.0.0, rich==13.9.4, platformdirs==4.3.6, typing-extensions==4.12.2) and pytest==8.3.4 is pinned when downloaded for the offline wheel cache used by test.sh. apt packages (git, curl, build-essential, libffi-dev, libssl-dev) are left unpinned, which is acceptable per the guidance."
  },
  "typos": {
    "outcome": "pass",
    "explanation": "Cross-checked task_id, commit hashes (both valid 40-character SHAs matching the real upstream repository), file paths, and test identifiers across task.toml, tests/config.json, tests/rubrics.json, test.sh, verifier.py, and judge.py — all consistent with no misspellings found. Test names in f2p/p2p match exactly what test.patch defines and what already exists in the base repository (verified test_content_parse_fail exists in the upstream base commit)."
  },
  "tests_or_solution_in_image": {
    "outcome": "pass",
    "explanation": "The Dockerfile comment states 'tests/ and solution/ are intentionally NOT copied into the image: the harness supplies them at verify time,' and indeed only environment/assets/ is COPYed in. No COPY or ADD instruction references tests/ or solution/."
  },
  "test_deps_in_image": {
    "outcome": "pass",
    "explanation": "pytest is only downloaded to /opt/test-wheels at build time (pip download, not pip install), and the Dockerfile even asserts the build fails if pytest ends up installed ('ERROR: pytest must not be installed in the image'). Actual installation happens in test.sh at verify time via --no-index --find-links=/opt/test-wheels, keeping the agent's image free of test-only tooling while still being offline/deterministic at verify time."
  },
  "hardcoded_solution": {
    "outcome": "pass",
    "explanation": "solution/solve.sh applies solution/fix.patch via git apply/patch, which is a genuine source-code change to src/textual/markup.py altering the span-ordering algorithm (reversing/sorting spans, guarding zero-width ranges). This was cross-verified against the real upstream PR #5829 commit history and matches it. It is a process-derived fix, not a printed/hardcoded answer."
  },
  "file_reference_mentioned": {
    "outcome": "not_applicable",
    "explanation": "The task requires fixing an existing source file (src/textual/markup.py) rather than producing new named output artifacts for the tests to check by filename. The agent is expected to locate the relevant file through normal code exploration, which is standard for a bug-fix task and not the file-production scenario this criterion targets."
  }
}

